---
/**
 * HOMEPAGE — this IS the live homepage, serving at /. The "preview route" wording
 * this line used to carry was left behind by the cutover and contradicted the line
 * beneath it.
 *
 * ── SCOPE: THE DEMO WORKSPACE IS CUT, NOT DEFERRED ────────────────────────────
 * Recorded here because there is no v3 plan doc — the plan has lived in PR bodies,
 * and an item that sits on a list forever is how the missing-visual count stayed
 * wrong for three PRs. CUT means it is off the roadmap; it is not waiting.
 *
 * The handoff's `demo-app` mount (demo-app.jsx + demo-screens.jsx +
 * full-dashboard.jsx + tweaks-panel.jsx + demo-data.jsx — ~120KB, ~2,753 lines,
 * 37 hooks) is NOT being ported. Two independent reasons, either sufficient:
 *
 *   1. §29.4 names it as the WRONG visual: "The hero visual should show an
 *      attribution story, not a generic dashboard." demo-app IS the six-screen KPI
 *      dashboard. MarketingInteractiveDemo, which §2 now renders, is the
 *      attribution story — and already exists, already truthful.
 *   2. TRUTH. Five capability assertions across FOUR of its six screens have never
 *      run: JourneyDrawer's "written to HubSpot and pushed to Google Ads"
 *      (demo-screens.jsx:195 — CRM sync has 0 references in api/, and
 *      capi_deliveries + ad_platform_connections are both 0); ScreenReports'
 *      "white-labelled" and "Scheduled to Slack" (:228-229 — white_label false on
 *      every tier; email-reports-weekly logs "Sent 0" every run); ScreenLeads' CRM
 *      chips (:124); and full-dashboard.jsx's "Best ROAS 6.2x" plus a ROAS column
 *      (:82,90 — §6 cost-gated, and there are 0 ad-platform connections).
 *      TrackingPanel was ALREADY rewritten for this reason; four more screens is a
 *      redesign, not a trim.
 *
 * CONSEQUENCES, stated so they are not rediscovered:
 *   · The Integrations and Leads SCREENS are not on the roadmap at all.
 *   · PR 6 is §11's `compare` alone.
 *   · FALLBACK if the query builder is later judged too dense for the hero: revert
 *     to JourneyMockup in §2 and §3 empty (the state before this change). NOT the
 *     port — that option is closed on the two grounds above.
 *
 * ── WHERE THE HANDOFF ACTUALLY IS ──────────────────────────────────────────────
 * `design_handoff_sourcetrack/` — 334 files, OUTSIDE VERSION CONTROL, which is why
 * "the design handoff" below cannot be resolved from this repo alone. Its README is
 * explicit: `source/lib/*.jsx` are Babel-in-browser PROTOTYPES and are NOT to be
 * lifted; the target stack is Astro, recreated as real Astro components. That
 * recreation covered the sections and copy and stopped at the visual layer — each
 * `data-mount` placeholder became a <p> describing the visual it replaced. 18
 * distinct mounts, 30 occurrences on the handoff's index.html.
 *
 * Built at /v3 so it can be measured and reviewed before any cutover decision.
 * Promoted from /v3 to / — indexable. This REPLACED the v2 homepage.
 *
 * RECREATED from the design handoff, never lifted. Header and Footer come from
 * Base.astro unchanged — one importer each serves all 36 pages, so v3 accepts the
 * existing chrome rather than forking it (§11 divergence).
 *
 * ── COPY PROVENANCE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Copy is the handoff's, verbatim, EXCEPT where it asserted something main cannot
 * evidence. Every substitution is listed in the PR body. Two categories:
 *
 *   1. Section 11 — four blocks CUT by ruling: server-side conversions to ad
 *      platforms (capi_deliveries: 0 rows all-time), CRM sync (0 references in
 *      api/), white-label reports (plan-features.js:71 — white_label false on
 *      every plan), scheduled/emailed reports (email-reports-weekly: 393 runs,
 *      "Sent 0" every one). Kept: first-party/server-side survival, and MCP.
 *
 *   2. Section 15 — plan features use the AUTHORITATIVE strings from live
 *      pricing.md, not the handoff's rewordings. The handoff says "All 9
 *      attribution models", "Cookieless, privacy-first tracking" and so on; 11 of
 *      its 13 strings are rewordings that the claims allowlist would reject. Most
 *      are true, one is not: "SEO revenue by keyword" implies query-level
 *      precision that §17.5 and §6 require be labelled ESTIMATED. Rather than
 *      widen the guard mid-build, the baseline strings ship. Flagged for a ruling.
 *
 * The AI count is 16 — settled against api/lib/channel-classifier.js's
 * AI_DOMAINS_MAP, which holds 16 distinct labels. The tracker's own client map
 * holds 13; the classifier is the source of truth because it is what the ingest
 * middleware, proxy and read side all resolve against.
 */
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import Wrap from "@/layouts/v3/Wrap.astro";
import SectionHead from "@/layouts/v3/SectionHead.astro";
import StatRow from "@/layouts/v3/StatRow.astro";
import Bento from "@/layouts/v3/Bento.astro";
import "@/styles/v3-tokens.css";
import "@/styles/v3-home.css";
import "@/styles/v4-type.css";
import "@/styles/v3-surfaces.css";

import HeroTicker from "@/components/v3/HeroTicker.jsx";
import CoverageTabs from "@/layouts/v3/surfaces/CoverageTabs.astro";
import LiveVisitors from "@/components/v3/LiveVisitors.jsx";
import PathBeam from "@/layouts/v3/surfaces/PathBeam.astro";
import CaptureIcon from "@/layouts/v3/surfaces/CaptureIcon.astro";
import ChannelMarquee from "@/layouts/v3/surfaces/ChannelMarquee.astro";
import ChipRow from "@/layouts/v3/surfaces/ChipRow.astro";
import JourneyChips from "@/layouts/v3/surfaces/JourneyChips.astro";
import ChannelTiles from "@/layouts/v3/surfaces/ChannelTiles.astro";
import MarkRow from "@/layouts/v3/surfaces/MarkRow.astro";
import SyncList from "@/layouts/v3/surfaces/SyncList.astro";
import ChannelMiniTable from "@/layouts/v3/surfaces/ChannelMiniTable.astro";
import CompareSlider from "@/components/v3/CompareSlider.jsx";
import MarketingInteractiveDemo from "@/components/MarketingInteractiveDemo";
import JourneyMockup from "@/components/JourneyMockup.astro";
import HeroStatCallout from "@/components/HeroStatCallout.astro";
import HowItWorksShowcase from "@/partials/HowItWorksShowcase.astro";
import UseCaseCards from "@/partials/UseCaseCards.astro";

// `icon` is a MONOGRAM, not a logo. §35.4 bans reconstructed third-party marks, and the
// handoff's own pattern is a letter or text label. These are OUR surface categories, so a
// letter tile is honest rather than standing in for a mark we may not draw.
// `tools` is the handoff's §6 tool-row: the integrations each surface captures from.
// Text labels only (§35.4) — the row is evidence for the card's claim, and a card that
// asserts coverage while showing none is the shell this closes.
const captureCards = [
  { icon: "F", h: "Forms", tools: ["HubSpot", "Typeform", "Webflow", "Gravity Forms"], p: "Every submission arrives with its source already attached — no hidden fields to wire up, no lost UTMs on multi-step forms." },
  { icon: "C", h: "Chat", tools: ["Intercom", "Drift", "Crisp"], p: "Conversations that start in a widget stop being anonymous. The chat lead inherits the session that led to it." },
  { icon: "M", h: "Meetings", tools: ["Calendly", "Cal.com", "Chili Piper"], p: "Know where a booked demo came from before you walk into it — source and campaign land on the calendar invite." },
  { icon: "S", h: "Signups", tools: ["Stripe", "Auth0", "Clerk"], p: "Trial starts and account signups classify as leads on arrival — signup, sign_up, trial and trial_start are all recognised by the taxonomy, so a self-serve funnel is attributed without extra wiring." },
  { icon: "P", h: "Payments", tools: ["Stripe", "Paddle", "Chargebee"], p: "Revenue, not just conversions. Each charge, upgrade, and renewal rolls up to the channel that originally earned it." },
  { icon: "O", h: "Store orders", tools: ["Shopify", "WooCommerce", "BigCommerce"], p: "Order-level attribution with LTV and repeat rate by acquisition source — the numbers your ad platform will never show you." },
];

const stats = [
  { value: "6", label: "conversion types captured — forms, chat, meetings, signups, payments, orders" },
  { value: "9", label: "attribution models running on one first-party dataset" },
  { value: "1", label: "script tag to install — no tag manager, no data engineer" },
  { value: "16", label: "AI assistants and answer engines classified on arrival" },
];

const faqs = [
  { q: "Do I have to replace GA4?", a: "No, and most teams don't. Keep it for traffic volume. SourceTrack runs alongside it and answers the question GA4 was never built for: which channel produced revenue." },
  { q: "Do I need to rebuild my forms?", a: "No. One script tag goes in your head, then you connect the tools you already use. Nothing gets rebuilt, and existing form submissions keep working while the source rides along." },
  { q: "What about visitors with no UTMs?", a: "That's the majority of real traffic. We use referrer data, click IDs, landing-page patterns, and known AI and answer-engine referrers to classify sessions that arrive untagged." },
  { q: "Why does AI traffic land in Direct everywhere else?", a: "Assistants and answer engines send stripped or unusual referrers, so conventional analytics dumps them into Direct. We fingerprint those patterns and label them." },
  { q: "How long does setup take?", a: "One script tag in your head, then connect a form source and watch the first session land." },
  { q: "My CRM already tracks source. Why this?", a: "Your CRM starts watching at the form submit, so everything before it — the ad, the answer engine, the three visits over two weeks — is invisible. SourceTrack records the path that led there." },
  { q: "Is it privacy-safe?", a: "Yes, and it's cookieless by default — anonymous ids in localStorage, no cross-site fingerprinting. Do Not Track and Global Privacy Control are honoured." },
];

// AUTHORITATIVE — from live pricing.md, not the handoff. See the header note.
const plans = [
  {
    name: "Starter", price: "$49", per: "/month", alt: "or $470 / year — two months free",
    blurb: "One site, finally getting an honest channel report.",
    features: ["250,000 tracked pageviews / mo", "Full Multi-Touch Attribution", "ChatGPT & AI Search Detection", "Cookieless Privacy-First Tracking", "Email & Chat Support"],
  },
  {
    name: "Growth", price: "$79", per: "/month", alt: "or $750 / year — two months free",
    blurb: "Running paid, and tired of three dashboards disagreeing.",
    features: ["1,000,000 tracked pageviews / mo", "Search Console SEO Revenue Attribution", "Stripe & Shopify Revenue Webhooks", "Visitor Journey Timeline Inspector"],
  },
  {
    name: "Founder Annual", price: "$99", per: "/year", alt: "Limited cohort while in beta",
    blurb: "Back the product early, keep the rate for good.",
    features: ["All Growth Plan Features Included", "1,000,000 tracked pageviews / mo", "Locked-in Founder Rate Forever", "Priority Founder Support", "Direct Founder Discord & Email Channel"],
  },
];

const comparison = [
  { what: "Pre-form touchpoints", crm: "Limited", ga4: "Lands in Direct", ft: "Partial", st: "Full journey" },
  { what: "Revenue by source", crm: "Partial", ga4: "Clicks only", ft: "Add-on", st: "First-party" },
  { what: "AI assistant traffic", crm: "Limited", ga4: "Direct", ft: "Last click", st: "Classified" },
  { what: "Time to first answer", crm: "Weeks, with onboarding", ga4: "Same day", ft: "Same day", st: "Same day" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Revenue attribution that shows which channels actually pay — SourceTrack"
  description="Know which channels actually make you money. One script tag, cookieless by default, nine attribution models on one first-party dataset."
>
  <div class="v3">
    {/* 1 — HERO */}
    <section class="v3-hero">
      <Wrap>
        <div class="v3-hero-meta">
          <span class="v3-pill">One script tag</span>
          <span class="v3-pill">From $49/mo</span>
          <span class="v3-pill">Cookieless by default</span>
        </div>
        <h1>Know which channels <em>actually make you money.</em></h1>
        <p class="v3-hero-sub">
          Every conversion carries its full path, first touch to payment, including AI
          tools your analytics can't see. One script tag.
        </p>
        {/* Orphaned by the cutover: Hero.astro was its only renderer and no longer ships.
            Re-homed rather than deleted — fixture-only, carries the §29.8 not-a-customer
            disclaimer, and four guards in homepage-fixture-sections.test.js were asserting
            against it while it rendered nowhere. */}
        {/* handoff `hero-ticker`. client:visible — it is below the fold on most
            viewports and there is no reason to hydrate it before it is seen. */}
        <HeroTicker client:visible />
        <HeroStatCallout />
          {/* Mechanism illustration, CSS-only. Placed in its OWN row beneath the hero
              copy — §2.6 forbids lime as a wash BEHIND primary content, so it sits
              adjacent, never underneath. It labels nothing and implies no delivery. */}
          <PathBeam />
      </Wrap>
    </section>

    {/* 2 — WINDOW-FRAME MOCKUP */}
    <section class="v3-section v3-section--tight">
      <Wrap>
        <div class="v3-frame">
          <div class="v3-frame-chrome">
            <span class="v3-frame-dots"><i /><i /><i /></span>
            <span class="v3-frame-url">app.sourcetrack.example / dashboard</span>
          </div>
          <div class="v3-frame-body">
            {/* §29.4: "The hero visual should show an attribution story, NOT a generic
                dashboard." That line is why the handoff's `demo-app` mount is NOT ported
                here — it is a six-screen KPI dashboard, i.e. exactly what §29.4 names as
                wrong. This component is literally an attribution story: pick a dimension,
                switch First↔Last Touch, watch attributed value move, open a stitched
                journey. Fixture-only and §29.5-clean (0 fetch/fetchApi/supabase/axios).
                JourneyMockup moved to §3, whose heading already promises a six-touchpoint
                journey and which rendered as a shell until now. */}
            <MarketingInteractiveDemo client:visible />
          </div>
        </div>
      </Wrap>
    </section>

    {/* handoff `marquee` mount — static by ruling; continuous scroll is PR 3. */}
    <ChannelMarquee />

    {/* 3 — frameless cards on paper */}
    <section class="v3-section">
      <Wrap>
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Customer journeys"
          heading="One customer. Six touchpoints. Six sources trying to take credit."
        />
          {/* Moved here from §2. #691 put it there only because §2 was an empty
              frame and §29.4 needed closing; this heading promises one customer and
              six touchpoints, so §3 stops being a heading-plus-paragraph shell. */}
          <JourneyMockup />
      </Wrap>
    </section>

    {/* 4 — COVERAGE. Card-on-paper, per the v4 design bundle.
        WAS `v3-section--dark`; the bundle renders this section on paper with a
        single bordered card. §2.7 (no two consecutive sections sharing a container
        treatment) still holds: §3 above is a window-frame mockup and §5 below is a
        window-frame mockup, and this sits between them as a tabbed card. */}
    <section class="v3-section">
      <Wrap>
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Coverage"
          heading="Attribution that works with the stack you already have."
          lede="Lead source tracking only matters if it reads the tools your conversions actually happen in. One script tag covers all of them — pick a surface to see the platforms."
        />
        {/* Replaces SurfaceTabs.jsx (React island, `client:visible`). Astro
            server-rendered its FIRST panel, so that much survived without JS — but
            the other four were reachable only through a click handler that never
            loaded. CoverageTabs is CSS-only: no island, all five panels reachable,
            native radio-group keyboard behaviour. Marks route through BrandMark, so
            each is an officially-sourced asset or a text label (§35.4). */}
        <CoverageTabs />
      </Wrap>
    </section>

    {/* 5 — WINDOW-FRAME MOCKUP (ruled) */}
    <section class="v3-section">
      <Wrap>
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Product tour"
          heading="Multi-touch attribution, in action."
          lede="Four surfaces that answer four different questions — who is on the site right now, where the revenue came from, which assistants are sending buyers, and what organic search is really worth."
        />
        <div class="v3-frame">
          <div class="v3-frame-chrome">
            <span class="v3-frame-dots"><i /><i /><i /></span>
            <span class="v3-frame-url">app.sourcetrack.example / attribution</span>
          </div>
          <div class="v3-frame-body">
              {/* handoff mounts for this section: channel-mini, ai-chips, tool-row.
                  The fourth, `live` (the realtime session feed), needs JS and is PR 3 —
                  it is absent here rather than faked with a still frame. */}
              <ChannelMiniTable />
              {/* The handoff closes §5 with a centred row carrying two 15px glyphs
                  (index.html:124-163). First-party marks, currentColor, so no paint value
                  lands in a style attribute (v3-page-pairs.mjs:552-553). */}
              <div class="v3-tourfoot">
                <a class="v3-link-quiet" href="/product">
                  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
                       stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                    <path d="M3 12h14" /><path d="M13 6l6 6-6 6" />
                  </svg>
                  See the full product tour
                </a>
                <a class="v3-link-quiet" href="/demo">
                  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="15" height="15" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
                       stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                    <path d="M12 3a9 9 0 100 18 9 9 0 000-18z" /><path d="M10 8.5l6 3.5-6 3.5z" />
                  </svg>
                  Open the live demo
                </a>
              </div>
              {/* handoff `live` — the fourth surface of this section, absent in PR 2
                  because it needs JS. Its tick moves a session between pages and
                  advances a timer; it NEVER invents a conversion (§6). */}
              <LiveVisitors client:visible />
              <div class="v3-frame-split">
                <ChipRow items={["ChatGPT", "Gemini", "Claude", "Perplexity", "Copilot"]} more="+11 more" />
                <ChipRow items={["HubSpot", "Salesforce", "Stripe", "Shopify"]} more="+8 more" />
              </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </Wrap>
    </section>

    {/* §29.3 positions 3 and 4 — "How it works" then the use-case cards.
        PORTED, not rebuilt: docs/design/design.md:2946-2954 mandates both on the homepage
        and the v3 draft shipped neither. §13 ("Who it's for") is thematically adjacent but
        carries ZERO links to /solutions/*, where v2 linked five — an internal-linking loss,
        not a styling difference. Placed HERE, straight after the product-preview cluster,
        because §29.3 orders "how it works" third; an explainer arriving after fourteen
        sections is not doing the job the spec assigns it.
        Both were migrated onto design-system tokens as part of this change — they carried
        #121616/#2D3333/#C7CFCF, pre-v1.3 values outside the token set, and shipping those
        on / is the defect #689 fixed elsewhere.
        Guarded by api/tests/homepage-fixture-sections.test.js. */}
    <HowItWorksShowcase />
    <UseCaseCards />

    {/* 6 — frameless cards on paper (non-adjacent to 3) */}
    <section class="v3-section">
      <Wrap>
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Capture"
          heading="Lead source tracking for every kind of conversion."
          lede="Buyers convert on whatever is in front of them — a chat widget at 11pm, a booking link on a phone, a Stripe checkout. SourceTrack attaches source, campaign and journey to each one."
        />
        <div class="v3-cards">
          {captureCards.map((c) => (
            <CaptureIcon name={c.h} />
            <div class="v3-card"><h3>{c.h}</h3><p>{c.p}</p></div>
            <div class="v3-toolrow">{c.tools.map((t) => <span class="v3-toolchip">{t}</span>)}</div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </Wrap>
    </section>

    {/* 7 — bordered stat band */}
    <section class="v3-section v3-section--tight">
      <Wrap><StatRow stats={stats} /></Wrap>
    </section>

    {/* 8 — soft band */}
    <section class="v3-section v3-section--soft">
      <Wrap>
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Measure"
          heading="Nine attribution models. One dataset underneath."
          lede="Attribution arguments are usually model arguments in disguise. First touch, last touch, linear, time decay, W-shaped, full path and more — the same dataset, re-credited."
        />
          {/* NINE models, named. The heading "Nine attribution models. One dataset
              underneath." is TRUE — ALLOWED_MODELS holds exactly 9
              (api/lib/report-config-validation.js:33) — so the heading stands and the
              visual supports it literally.
              NO revenue and NO percentages: ModelCompareMockup was NOT wired here even
              though it exists, because it shows THREE models under a nine-model heading
              (a contradiction a reader can count) and carries $240/$80 figures — the same
              derivation that got bento-attr cut. Names and families derive nothing. */}
          <div class="v3-models">
            {[
              { fam: "Touch-based", items: ["First touch", "Last touch", "First touch (non-direct)", "Last touch (non-direct)"] },
              { fam: "Multi-touch", items: ["Linear", "U-shaped", "W-shaped", "Time decay"] },
              { fam: "AI-aware",    items: ["AI platforms"] },
            ].map(({ fam, items }) => (
              <div class="v3-models-fam">
                <span class="v3-models-label">{fam}</span>
                <div class="v3-models-list">
                  {items.map((m) => <span class="v3-models-chip">{m}</span>)}
                </div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
      </Wrap>
    </section>

    {/* 9 — plain */}
    <section class="v3-section">
      <Wrap>
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="The GA4 problem"
          heading="The GA4 alternative built for revenue, not pageviews."
          lede="Compare a default GA4 report with the SourceTrack revenue view. Same week, same traffic — but one of them can tell you which channel paid for itself."
        />
          {/* handoff `compare`. TWO of its four SourceTrack tiles were REPLACED, not
              ported: it headlines "AI Search revenue" and "Best conversion rate", and
              ai_revenue + conversion_rate are both in GATED_METRICS — unservable at any
              tier (422), not plan-gating. conversions and leads were verified against
              GATED_METRICS *and* ALLOWED_METRICS before use. See KI-115. */}
          <CompareSlider client:visible />
      </Wrap>
    </section>

    {/* 10 — soft band + bento */}
    <section class="v3-section v3-section--soft">
      <Wrap>
        <SectionHead
          eyebrow="Everything in one tracker"
          heading="Source. Journey. Conversion. Revenue."
          lede="Every block is a real report you can pin, rebuild, or send to a client — running on the same dataset as the demo workspace above."
        />
          {/* handoff `channel-tiles` — monogram tiles, §35.4: never a drawn mark. */}
          <ChannelTiles />
        <Bento>
          <div class="v3-bento-cell v3-bento-cell-dark" style="grid-column: span 7">
            <h3>Stitch first touch, last touch, assists, and revenue into one view.</h3>
            <p>Attribution engine</p>
            {/* Replaces the handoff's `bento-attr` mount, which derived revenue from
                fixtures (D.modelRevenue) and was cut in PR 2 on §6 grounds. The CELL is
                honest — nine models over one dataset is true (ALLOWED_MODELS,
                api/lib/report-config-validation.js:33) — so it is refilled rather than
                dropped. What is shown is the SHAPE of a credit split and the model names:
                no amounts, no percentages, nothing derived. The bars are illustrative
                weighting only, which is why they carry no numeric label. */}
            <div class="v3-modelsplit">
              {[
                { m: "First touch", w: [70, 15, 15] },
                { m: "Last touch",  w: [15, 15, 70] },
                { m: "Linear",      w: [34, 33, 33] },
                { m: "U-shaped",    w: [40, 20, 40] },
              ].map(({ m, w }) => (
                <div class="v3-modelsplit-row">
                  <span class="v3-modelsplit-name">{m}</span>
                  <span class="v3-modelsplit-bar" aria-hidden="true">
                    {w.map((x) => <i style={`flex:${x}`} />)}
                  </span>
                </div>
              ))}
              <p class="v3-modelsplit-foot">…and five more, over the same dataset.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="v3-bento-cell v3-bento-cell-accent" style="grid-column: span 5">
            <h3>AI referrals are the channel ad platforms can't see.</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="v3-bento-cell" style="grid-column: span 5">
            <h3>Report builder</h3>
            <p>Pick a template, name it, pin it. The preview updates as you type.</p>
            {/* The handoff's three inline rows (index.html:294-298). Ported as classes,
                not inline styles — a paint value in style="" fails
                v3-page-pairs.mjs:552-553 and is invisible to the contrast audit. The lime
                row is the pinned one; §2.6 caps accent density, so exactly one row carries
                it. No revenue figure — the handoff's read "$21,430", which is the same
                derived-number problem as bento-attr. */}
            <div class="v3-rb">
              <span class="v3-rb-row v3-rb-row--pinned"><b>Channel revenue</b><i>PINNED</i></span>
              <span class="v3-rb-row"><b>AI referral leads</b><i>PIN</i></span>
              <span class="v3-rb-row"><b>Campaign ROAS</b><i>PIN</i></span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="v3-bento-cell" style="grid-column: span 4">
            <h3>One script tag</h3>
            <p>Drop it in your head, via GTM or your own tag. No data engineer required.</p>
            {/* ⚠️ NOT the handoff's snippet. index.html:304 ships
                `cdn.sourcetrack.io/st.js` — a CDN that does not exist; porting it verbatim
                would put a non-functional install line on the homepage. The canonical
